            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Your home is very leaky at 19.44 ACH. The biggest leaks are at the old woodstove chimney (try caulking around the gap) and the forced air furnace. If you are not using the furnace and have no plans to in the future you should consider removing it and sealing the chimney, ducting and the floor grates. A friend did this a few years ago and now has an extra closet. Behind the closet in the small bedroom where the hot water tank used to be was quite leaky. Sealing the plumbing penetrations throught the floor would help. Both the stove and bathroom fans are leaky. Check and possibly replace the exterior hoods. The ceiling light fixtures and outlets on exterior walls can be fitted with foam gaskets that go behind the face plates. Many of the windows leak around the frames and or around the panes of glass (especially the sliding windows) Replace the windows or remove the trim around the windows and use a spray foam to seal around the frames.  </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>As an upgrade I added 2 inches of rigid foam to the outside of your walls. This may also help reduce the leakiness of the house.</mainWalls>
                <windows>The windows are the second largest area (along with the walls) of heat loss and contribute to the leakiness of the house so I replaced  seven of them that are only double paned with triple paned, LowE, argon filled units with insulating spacers.</windows>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
